.TH "rbcBindings" 3tcl 0\&.5 "tclsimrawreader" "Tcl NgspiceTclBridge package"
.SH NAME
rbcBindings - Commands in namespace rbcBindings
.SH "RBC VECTORS SUPPORT"
.SH "OPTIONAL RBC VECTOR OUTPUT"
.PP
The default build and output remain Tcl lists (or dictionaries of lists), with no RBC dependency\&. To enable
real and complex \fIrbc-tk9\fP [URL: https://github\&.com/georgtree/rbc-tk9] output, use Tcl 9 and RBC 0\&.8\&.1 or newer
headers and runtime:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&./configure --with-tcl=/path/to/tcl/lib --with-rbc=/path/to/rbc-tk9
make clean
make
.fi
.RE
.PP
--with-rbc accepts a source tree, installation prefix, or directory containing rbcVector\&.h, rbcDecls\&.h and
rbcStubLib\&.c, just as in tclmeasure\&. The portable stub client is compiled into this package; the RBC shared
library is not linked\&. --without-rbc selects the default list-only build\&. Even an enabled build can load and
read lists without RBC installed\&. rbc::vector is required only when vector output is selected\&. No Tk
initialization is needed, although the installed RBC binary must have its system library dependencies available\&.
.PP
Set handle defaults with -output list|vector and -ifexists error|replace\&. The initial defaults are list and error; omitted -output and -ifexists at open time inherit the package configuration\&.
Either option can be overridden for a single vector or vectors call, without changing the handle defaults:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set raw [::tclsimrawreader::openraw file\&.raw -output vector -ifexists error -namespace ::wave]
set signal [$raw vector v(out)]
# -> ::wave::v(out)
set values [$signal index :]
set commands [$raw vectors {time v(out)} -ifexists replace]
# -> time ::wave::time v(out) ::wave::v(out)
set values [$raw vector v(out) -output list]
$raw close
# Created vectors remain alive and are owned by the caller\&.
.fi
.RE
.PP
For openraw, options may precede or follow the file name\&. For read commands, -output, -ifexists and -namespace may
precede or follow the selection; -plot precedes it as before\&. Existing -from, -count, selected-name
lists, -all, dialects and multi-plot handling work in both output modes\&. In vector mode, vector returns one
fully qualified command name; vectors returns a dictionary whose original raw-name keys map to fully
qualified command names\&.  An empty selection returns an empty dictionary\&. A zero-length range creates an empty
vector\&.
.PP
An explicit openraw -namespace name selects a fixed destination for all vector reads\&. Relative names resolve
against the opening caller; missing namespaces and parents are created at open time\&. The qualified name is
retained for reads from other namespaces\&. Without this option, destinations use the read caller as before\&.
A per-read -namespace name on vector or vectors overrides the handle setting for that call only\&.
It requires effective vector output, resolves relative to the read caller, and creates missing parents\&.
For example: $raw vectors -all -output vector -namespace ::snapshots -names #auto\&.
Empty namespace names are rejected\&. This option also works with list defaults and later -output vector
overrides\&. Closing the handle deletes neither the namespace nor its caller-owned output vectors\&.
.SS "DESTINATION NAMES AND OWNERSHIP"
.PP
Raw names are preserved whenever they are safe literal RBC names\&. Balanced parentheses, underscores, periods,
letters, digits, @ and single interior colons are retained\&. Vectors are created with -literal true, so
numeric names such as v(1) or v(1)(2:4) are never interpreted as length/range specifications\&. Examples:
.PP
.nf
┌────────────┬─────────────────────────┐
│ Raw name   │ Destination tail        │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
│ time       │ time                    │
│ v(out)     │ v(out)                  │
│ v(1)       │ v(1)                    │
│ v_28out_29 │ v_28out_29              │
│ L2:flux    │ L2:flux                 │
│ v(a-b)     │ _raw_7628612D6229       │
│ sub::node  │ _raw_7375623A3A6E6F6465 │
└────────────┴─────────────────────────┘

.fi
.PP
Names with unsupported characters, unbalanced parentheses, ::, a leading/trailing colon, or the reserved
prefix _raw_ use a fallback: _raw_ followed by the uppercase hexadecimal UTF-8 bytes of the complete
original name\&.  The empty name maps to _raw_\&. Reserving this prefix keeps fallback names distinct from
literal names; a raw name that already begins with _raw_ is itself encoded\&. Original dictionary keys are
always retained\&.
.PP
The destination uses the per-call namespace, then the handle namespace, then the read caller namespace\&. Raw namespace separators are
encoded, so a raw name cannot select a different namespace\&. Use
$raw vectors -all -output vector -namespace ::signals to choose a namespace explicitly
(create it first)\&. Newly created vectors have no mapped Tcl array variable: use the returned command, for
example $signal index 1:20, rather than Tcl array substitution $v(out)(1:20)\&.  New vector indexes start at
zero, including partial reads\&. Closing a handle does not destroy its output vectors; use rbc::vector destroy
when done\&.
.PP
This naming replaces the earlier encoding of every parenthesis and underscore\&. Existing vectors are not renamed\&.
Use returned command names or dictionary values rather than reconstructing names using the old encoding\&.
.SS "COLLISIONS AND NUMERIC TYPES"
.PP
-ifexists error rejects an existing vector or command\&. -ifexists replace replaces the data of an existing
RBC vector of the same numeric type, retaining the vector identity, clients, index offset and any existing
array mapping\&. It never overwrites an unrelated Tcl command\&. RBC vector types are immutable: a real/complex
mismatch is an error even with replace; explicitly destroy the old vector first if changing type is intended\&.
.PP
Each variable's resolved raw storage determines its RBC type\&. Complex samples become complex RBC vectors without
losing their imaginary components\&. Real samples (including real axes in mixed layouts) remain real vectors\&.
Numeric data is decoded into buffers directly, without a full intermediate Tcl list\&. All selected data is read
and all known name/type collisions are checked before publication, so read and preflight errors leave existing
values unchanged\&. RBC notifications and command traces may execute application callbacks during publication;
updates are not a transaction against reentrant application code\&.
.SH "READ-ONLY DESTINATIONS"
.PP
-ifexists replace preserves the read-only state and never unlocks a destination\&. A locked real or complex
output fails with RBC VECTOR READONLY\&. Every selected destination is checked before reading/publishing
results, so a pre-existing lock on a later column leaves earlier vectors unchanged\&. Permission is checked
again during publication to handle callbacks\&. List output remains available regardless of destination locks\&.
Unlock explicitly before replacing, or request a fresh destination / #auto\&. Newly created vectors are writable\&.
.SH "AUTOMATIC AND EXPLICIT OUTPUT VECTOR NAMES"
.PP
For a single read, use -name #auto with vector output\&. For a bulk read, use -names #auto to allocate a
fresh RBC vector for every selected signal:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set raw [::tclsimrawreader::openraw file\&.raw -namespace ::results]
set one [$raw vector v(out) -output vector -name #auto]
set waves [$raw vectors -all -output vector -names #auto]
# Original raw names remain dictionary keys; values are actual RBC command names\&.
set output [dict get $waves v(out)]
puts [$output length]
::rbc::vector destroy $one {*}[dict values $waves]
$raw close
.fi
.RE
.PP
-names also accepts a dictionary mapping selected raw names to destinations, for example
-names {time #auto v(out) saved}\&. Omitted entries retain the normal signal-derived naming\&.
-name belongs to vector; -names belongs to vectors\&. Both require vector output and may precede or
follow the selection\&. They are per-read options, not openraw defaults\&.  Unknown or unselected keys, duplicate
selections/destinations, and empty destinations are errors\&.
.PP
Relative destinations, including #auto, use the per-call -namespace, the handle's namespace, or the read
caller's namespace, in that order\&. Omitted -names entries use signal-derived names in the selected namespace\&. An absolute destination overrides this location; its parent namespace must exist\&. RBC
itself expands a final #auto component in the selected namespace: the reader does not generate names or
counters\&. auto without # is an ordinary explicit name\&.  Repeated automatic reads create distinct vectors
even with -ifexists replace; explicit names retain the existing collision/type policy\&. Real and complex
output, empty ranges, and lack of mapped Tcl arrays behave as before\&. Returned vectors outlive the raw handle
and are owned by the caller\&. If publication fails, vectors created by that read are removed\&.
.SH "PACKAGE DEFAULTS"
.PP
Set representation and collision defaults once after loading the package:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
package require tclsimrawreader
::tclsimrawreader::configure -output vector -ifexists replace
.fi
.RE
.PP
New handles inherit output and collision defaults unless their creation call supplies -output or -ifexists explicitly\&. Existing
handles keep their settings; per-read overrides still take precedence\&.
.PP
Use ::tclsimrawreader::configure to query all settings, or ::tclsimrawreader::configure -output to query one\&.
The all-settings dictionary can be restored with ::tclsimrawreader::configure {*}$saved\&.
Initial representation defaults remain list; the initial -ifexists policy is error\&. Invalid requests and unavailable vector support leave settings unchanged\&.
Configuration belongs to the current Tcl interpreter\&. The command is public but \fBnot namespace-exported\fP,
so wildcard imports cannot introduce a configure collision\&. Namespace selection and naming are unchanged\&. -ifexists replace still requires a compatible, writable destination\&.
The collision setting affects vector output only and can be configured even in a build without RBC\&.


